<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cycloartenol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>Oxidosqualene cyclases (OSCs) are among the most conserved sterol bosynthetic enzymes at the sequence level, and homologues have been detected in all species capable of sterol synthesis. There are two main types of sterols-producing OSCs based on the end product of the cyclization: |FRAME: EC-5.4.99.7 EC 5.4.99.7, lanosterol synthase|, which is found in animals, fungi, choanozoa, trypanosomatids, dinoflagellates, and some bacteria such as |FRAME: TAX-414 &lt;i&gt;Methylococcus capsulatus&lt;/i&gt;|; and |FRAME: EC-5.4.99.8 EC 5.4.99.8, cycloartenol synthase|, which is found in higher plants, red and green algae, amoebozoa, diatoms, euglenids, heterolobosea and some bacteria such as |FRAME: TAX-41 &lt;i&gt;Stigmatella aurantiaca&lt;/i&gt;| (see |FRAME: PWY-8028 cycloartenol biosynthesis|) |CITS: [12519197][24596261]|. A rare third type is |FRAME: EC-5.4.99.47 EC 5.4.99.47, parkeol synthase| |CITS: [21526825]|. The reaction catalyzed by OSCs is remarkably complex |CITS: [16754609]|. The key steps in the cyclization include: 1. Positioning of squalene 2,3-epoxide in a prefolded configuration within the active site. 2. Protonation of the epoxide group by a catalytic acid. 3. Ring formation, during which cation intermediates are stabilized by cation&amp;ndash;&amp;pi; interactions with aromatic residues of the active site, followed by migration of the cation out to C20. 4. Skeletal rearrangement via hydride- and methyl-shifts (largely spontaneous) as the proton migrates back to a region of high &amp;pi;-electron density around the B/C rings. 5. Deprotonation by basic residues to quench the final protosteryl cation; the position of the deprotonation determines the OSC product (lanosterol versus cycloartenol). It has been proposed that the ancestral sterol biosynthesis pathway produced cycloartenol as a final product, and that the evolution of the pathway beyond cycloartenol took place only as a result of the rising concentrations of oxygen in the atmosphere following the onset of photosynthesis |CITS: [9383366]|.</Description>
